        <article class="help-text">
            <p class="lead">
                GS Test Harness checks a telescope server for ASCOM compliance: <strong>COM driver</strong>, or
                <strong>Alpaca server</strong>. It calls every ITelescopeV4 member (and, for
                Alpaca, the HTTP protocol), and for each problem says what's wrong, what ASCOM expects, how to fix it and
                where the code to change probably is.
            </p>
            <p class="lead">New here? Start with the <a href="help#quick-start">Quick start</a>: one picture of the whole job, from adding a server to a clean report.</p>

            <section id="quick-start">
                <h2>Quick start: from the first run to a clean report</h2>
                <p>The whole job in one picture. Follow the numbers from top to bottom. The loop in <strong>E</strong> repeats until the report is
                    clean. Every step says where to click; the sections below explain each part in full.</p>

                <div class="flow" role="group" aria-label="Workflow from setting up a server to a clean report">

                    <div class="flow-phase phase-a">
                        <h3><span class="flow-letter">A</span> Set up <small>once for each server</small></h3>
                        <ol class="flow-steps">
                            <li><span class="flow-n">1</span><b>Add your server</b>
                                <span>On the left list: <b>+</b> to type it in, the magnifier to find Alpaca servers on the network, or the telescope for the ASCOM drivers on this PC.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">2</span><b>Put it on the Server card</b>
                                <span>On the <b>Run</b> tab, drag the server onto the Server card (or select it and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>).</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">3</span><b>Check its settings</b>
                                <span>Under the server: Mount (simulator or real), Site hemisphere, flip angle, timeouts and the source folder. The checklist marks anything that looks wrong with ⚠.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">4</span><b>Choose what a run may do</b>
                                <span>On the <b>Options</b> tab. Tick <b>Move the mount</b> only for a simulator, or a real mount that is clear to move with the OTAs removed.</span></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>

                    <div class="flow-down" aria-hidden="true">▼</div>

                    <div class="flow-phase phase-b">
                        <h3><span class="flow-letter">B</span> Run tier 1 <small>the full check</small></h3>
                        <ol class="flow-steps">
                            <li><span class="flow-n">5</span><b>Click Start run</b>
                                <span>Beside the server on the Run tab. The <b>Safety Warning!</b> dialog opens.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">6</span><b>Put the mount at home</b>
                                <span>If it isn't, click <b>Go home</b>, or put it there by hand, power it OFF and ON, and click <b>Check for home</b>. Read the points, then click <b>Start run</b>.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">7</span><b>Watch the run</b>
                                <span><b>Run Status</b> shows the group it is in. If something looks unsafe it pauses and asks <b>Proceed</b>, <b>Skip</b> or <b>Stop</b>. <b>Stop run</b> in the header ends it any time.</span></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>

                    <div class="flow-down" aria-hidden="true">▼</div>

                    <div class="flow-phase phase-c">
                        <h3><span class="flow-letter">C</span> Read the results</h3>
                        <ol class="flow-steps">
                            <li><span class="flow-n">8</span><b>Look at the report</b>
                                <span>Filter by Fail, Error or Warning. Open a finding to see what ASCOM expects, what the server did and how to fix it. <b>Open report</b> and <b>Markdown</b> keep it.</span></li>
                        </ol>
                        <div class="flow-decision">
                            <b>Problems found?</b>
                            <span class="flow-branch yes"><b>Yes</b> &rarr; carry on to D</span>
                            <span class="flow-branch no"><b>No</b> &rarr; you are done: go to F</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="flow-down" aria-hidden="true">▼</div>

                    <div class="flow-phase phase-d">
                        <h3><span class="flow-letter">D</span> Find the cause <small>deeper tiers</small></h3>
                        <ol class="flow-steps">
                            <li><span class="flow-n">9</span><b>Tier 2 starts by itself</b>
                                <span>When tier 1 finishes the mount goes home and tier 2 begins, with no second warning. It measures the problems tier 1 found and narrows down the cause.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">10</span><b>Tier 3 follows</b>
                                <span>After tier 2 the mount goes home again. The deepest tier confirms the cause and gives the fix, with the code to change. Not every problem has a deeper test. A tier with too many (or too important) failures is not followed by the next one, for safety.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">&#10003;</span><b>The finished dialog</b>
                                <span>At the end the mount goes home and a dialog lists each tier's report and the <b>suggested new tests</b>; tick the ones you want and download them as Markdown.</span></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>

                    <div class="flow-down" aria-hidden="true">▼</div>

                    <div class="flow-phase phase-e">
                        <h3><span class="flow-letter">E</span> Fix it and prove it <small>repeat until clean</small></h3>
                        <ol class="flow-steps">
                            <li><span class="flow-n">11</span><b>Collect the fixes</b>
                                <span><b>Reviews</b> lists each problem's fix and where in the code. <b>Summary</b> lets you tick problem areas and <b>Download Markdown</b> for an AI agent or a developer.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">12</span><b>Change the server's code</b>
                                <span>Make the fixes, then rebuild and restart the server.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">13</span><b>Run it again</b>
                                <span>Click <b>Re-run tier 1</b> (all the tests again), or <b>Re-run tier 2 or 3</b> to check one diagnosis without waiting for the whole run.</span></li>
                        </ol>
                        <div class="flow-decision">
                            <b>All clean now?</b>
                            <span class="flow-branch yes"><b>Yes</b> &rarr; go to F</span>
                            <span class="flow-branch no"><b>No</b> &#8634; back to step 9 for what is left, then 11 to 13 again</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="flow-down" aria-hidden="true">▼</div>

                    <div class="flow-phase phase-f">
                        <h3><span class="flow-letter">F</span> Finish <small>keep the evidence</small></h3>
                        <ol class="flow-steps">
                            <li><span class="flow-n">14</span><b>Keep the report</b>
                                <span>Every run is saved with a name that starts with its tier, such as <b>1-RAC0012</b>. Reopen it on <b>Reports</b>, or download it as Markdown.</span></li>
                            <li><span class="flow-n">15</span><b>Check it automatically</b>
                                <span>Run the same check from the command line in a build: <code>GS.TestHarness --run Alpaca …</code>. Exit code 0 means nothing failed.</span></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <p><strong>Starting from a ConformU debug log?</strong> Use the <b>ConformU Debug Log Test</b> tab instead of steps 5 to 7: drag the server on,
                    drop the log on the card beside it and click <b>Start run</b>. Only the members ConformU flagged are checked, and C to F are the same.</p>

                <h3>Any time</h3>
                <ul class="flow-anytime">
                    <li><b>Stop run</b> in the header ends a run and aborts any slew, from any page.</li>
                    <li><b>Quick start</b> and the <b>?</b> button open this help. The (?) beside a setting opens its part.</li>
                    <li><b>Day, Dark, Dusk, Night</b> and <b>Custom</b> change the colours. Night is red only, to keep your night vision at the telescope.</li>
                    <li>Under <b>Options</b>, switch on the sound and notification to be told when a run finishes or needs your answer.</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="start">
                <h2>Getting started</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li>Add the server to the <strong>Servers</strong> list on the left (see <a href="help#servers">The server list</a>).</li>
                    <li>Drag it onto the <strong>Server Selection</strong> card on the <strong>Run</strong> tab (or select it and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>).</li>
                    <li>Check the server's <strong>Settings</strong> (the checklist under it marks anything that looks wrong with ⚠), choose what the run may do under
                        <strong>Options</strong>, and press <strong>Start run</strong> beside the server. The <strong>Safety Warning!</strong> dialog asks you to put the
                        mount at home, to remove the OTAs and to watch the mount for the whole run. Clicking <strong>Start run</strong> there accepts responsibility for the run.</li>
                    <li>Read the findings as they come in. Each run is saved; open it later on the <strong>Reports</strong> tab. For a picture of the whole job, see the
                        <a href="help#quick-start">Quick start</a>.</li>
                </ol>
                <p class="warning">Moving the mount is off by default. Turn it on only for a simulator, or a real mount that's clear to move.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="servers">
                <h2>The server list</h2>
                <p>The buttons above the list:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Add (+)</dt>
                    <dd>A new entry: a name, which server it is, and its Alpaca host, port and device number (with a user
                        name and password for a server that asks for one), or the COM driver's ProgID.</dd>
                    <dt>Alpaca Discovery (magnifier)</dt>
                    <dd>Searches the local network for Alpaca telescopes and lists the ones that answer. Pick one to add it.</dd>
                    <dt>ASCOM Drivers (telescope)</dt>
                    <dd>Lists the ASCOM telescope drivers installed on this computer. Windows only.</dd>
                    <dt>Connect (plug)</dt>
                    <dd>Connects to, or disconnects from, the selected server, to see that it answers. A run connects by itself.</dd>
                    <dt>Save (disk)</dt>
                    <dd>Saves the list now. It's also saved after every change.</dd>
                    <dt>Delete (bin)</dt>
                    <dd>Removes the selected server from the list. Its reports are kept.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>The dot before a server is green while it's connected. Double-click a server (or press <kbd>F2</kbd>)
                    to edit it, and press <kbd>Delete</kbd> to remove it. The <strong>«</strong> button folds the list
                    into a narrow strip; this browser remembers the choice.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="run">
                <h2>The Run tab</h2>
                <p>Three tabs run across the top: <strong>ASCOM Conformance Check</strong> (a full check),
                    <strong>ConformU Debug Log Test</strong> (following up a ConformU debug log, see <a href="help#conformu">ConformU Debug Log Test</a>)
                    and <strong>Options</strong> (what a run may do, for both). Under <strong>ASCOM Conformance Check</strong> a second
                    row holds its pages: <strong>Run</strong>, <strong>Reports</strong>,
                    <strong>Reviews</strong> and <strong>Summary</strong>. Under <strong>ConformU Debug Log Test</strong>
                    the same row holds the pages its runs use: <strong>Run</strong>, <strong>Reports</strong>,
                    <strong>Reviews</strong> and <strong>Summary</strong>. Each tab's pages
                    show only its own runs: under ConformU Debug Log Test, Reports, Reviews and Summary show only ConformU runs
                    (the ones that followed up a log); under ASCOM Conformance Check, they show only full conformance runs. A report's reference starts with its tier (<strong>1</strong>, <strong>2</strong> or <strong>3</strong>), then says which test made it:
                    <strong>RAC</strong> (ASCOM Conformance Check, as 1-RAC0031) or <strong>RCU</strong> (ConformU Debug Log Test, as 1-RCU0034). A
                    tier 2 run of the same device is 2-RAC0032, a tier 3 run 3-RAC0033. Older references without the tier (RAC0031) still find the report. The check groups a run makes are listed under
                    <a href="help#checks">What gets checked</a>, and every check in them under <a href="help#tests">The tests</a>.</p>
                <p>The <strong>Server Selection</strong> card holds the server to test. Drag one onto it from the list;
                    <strong>✕</strong> clears it. Under the server, on both tests, a <strong>Settings</strong> section (closed
                    until you open it, with a reminder to check it before starting a run) holds these:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Source Code local Path or GitHub full URL to the code</dt>
                    <dd>Where the code of the server selected is: a folder on this computer or a GitHub URL. Leave it blank if
                        the code isn't available for review. It lets findings name a file (see
                        <a href="help#source">Where to check the code</a>).</dd>
                    <dt>Mount</dt>
                    <dd>What the server drives: <strong>Simulator</strong> or a <strong>Real mount</strong>. GSServer takes
                        different code paths for each, so the ConformU diagnoses' recommendations follow it; not specified, they
                        list both. It's remembered as soon as it's changed. On the command line, <code>--mount simulator</code>
                        or <code>--mount real</code>.</dd>
                    <dt>Flip angle</dt>
                    <dd>Degrees either side of the meridian (0 to 90) where the mount can point from either side of the pier.
                        Inside it the flip checks flip the mount with the SideOfPier command, halfway in on each side, and it must
                        keep pointing at the same place. A goto 5° past it, across the meridian, must flip the mount by itself.
                        Needs <em>Move the mount</em> (under Options). Remembered as soon as it is changed. 0 (the default) when
                        it isn't known: there's no zone, so the SideOfPier flip isn't checked.</dd>
                    <dt>Slew timeout and call timeout</dt>
                    <dd>How long a slew, and any single call, may take before it counts as never answered. Remembered as soon as they are changed.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>Under the server a short <strong>checklist</strong> shows what to know before you start: ✓ for what is fine, ⚠ for what looks wrong (the
                    Mount not set, a short timeout for a real mount, no source folder) and ℹ for information (whether Move the mount is on, the Site hemisphere, whether it is
                    connected).</p>
                <p><strong>Start run</strong> sits beside the server; a <a href="help#safety">safety warning</a> comes first. While a run goes, the
                    <strong>Run Status</strong> bar shows its status beside the title, with <strong>Stop</strong> to end it early (the mount is still put back), the progress, the time
                    it has been going and how long the last run of the device took. Under that, the three tiers show where the run is, and what can come next (see
                    <a href="help#tiers">Diagnostic tiers</a>). Tiers 2 and 3 start by themselves, with the mount sent home between them and at the end. When
                    everything is done a dialog says so and lists the suggested new tests.</p>
                <p>The <strong>Report</strong> section below it holds the findings as they come in, with their filters. When the run is done, <strong>Open report</strong> and
                    <strong>Markdown</strong> at the top of it open or download the report.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="conformu">
                <h2>ConformU Debug Log Test</h2>
                <p>Drop a ConformU debug log on the card beside the Server Selection card, or click it to choose one.
                    It must be a debug log: run ConformU with debug logging turned on in its settings. A normal log (one
                    with no DEBUG lines) is turned away with a note saying so. Every ERROR, ISSUE, CONFIGURATION ALERT and WARNING line is read (the repeats in ConformU's
                    end-of-run summary count once) and grouped by the ITelescopeV4 member it is about. The table shows, for
                    each, what ConformU said and which harness checks look at it.</p>
                <p><strong>Start run</strong>, beside the server, checks only the members ConformU found problems
                    with. It runs the groups that check them, and within each group only the parts that touch those members
                    (for a PulseGuide problem, Motion's pulse-guide moves but not its slews, syncs or flips), plus the quick
                    read-only groups they depend on (Connection, Common, Capabilities, Properties, Methods, Performance).
                    Only findings about the problem members are reported, with anything that explains a check not made. Extended checks
                    are turned on when a problem needs them; moving the mount and writing settings stay as set under
                    <strong>Options</strong>, and the card says which problems they would leave unchecked.</p>
                <p>The <strong>ConformU</strong> findings then give a verdict for each problem:</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Reproduced (Fail or Warning)</dt>
                    <dd>A harness check of the same member found a problem: ConformU's problem is real, and the harness's fix applies.
                        For a member the harness diagnoses (see Diagnoses below), only the diagnosis counts: it found the cause in the driver.</dd>
                    <dt>Explained (Warning)</dt>
                    <dd>Diagnosed members only: the member works, and ConformU's result comes from something else, such as a stale or
                        coarse position reading. The diagnosis's recommendation applies.</dd>
                    <dt>Not reproduced (Info)</dt>
                    <dd>Every harness check of the member passed, or its diagnosis found it within tolerance: fixed since the ConformU run,
                        or ConformU tested something the harness doesn't.</dd>
                </dl>
                <p>For a diagnosed member, other problems the harness's checks find with it (a wrong error type, say) are listed in the
                    verdict as <em>also found</em>: they're real, but they aren't the problem ConformU reported.</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Not checked (Skipped)</dt>
                    <dd>The checks that would look at it didn't run (motion off, for example). Turn it on and run again.</dd>
                    <dt>Generated check (Probe)</dt>
                    <dd>A property no harness check reached in this run is read three times, and its values, errors and read times reported.</dd>
                    <dt>No check (Warning)</dt>
                    <dd>Nothing here can test it safely (a ConformU-only topic): reproduce it by hand.</dd>
                </dl>
                <h3>Diagnoses</h3>
                <p>This run is tier 1: it finds the problems. Rate offset and pulse guide problems can then be taken further
                    at tiers 2 and 3 (see <a href="help#tiers">Diagnostic tiers</a>), whose findings
                    (<strong>ConformU.Diagnose…</strong>) decide the verdict for those members.</p>
                <p>Set <strong>Mount</strong> under the server (on the Server card) so the recommendations point at the right
                    code: GSServer's simulator (GS.Simulator) or a real mount (GS.SkyWatcher). Not specified, they list both.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="tiers">
                <h2>Diagnostic tiers</h2>
                <p>Both tests go in tiers, each deeper than the one before. Tier 1 always runs first (<strong>Start run</strong>, on either test,
                    is tier 1); tiers 2 and 3 then run by themselves, only for
                    problems the tier below found that have a deeper test. Each tier makes its own report, and they are grouped together.</p>
                <p>The mount is sent home after every tier and at the end. Each test has a <a href="help#weights">weight</a> from 1 to 10 (10: a test that can't fail, such as the connection or
                    stopping the mount). A tier is not followed by the next if one of those weight-10 tests failed, or if its failed tests' weights add up to more than
                    @Tiers.MaxProblemsToContinue; the finished dialog then says it stopped for safety. To check a fix, run the tests again.
                    A tier 2 or 3 report is named with its tier first, such as <strong>2-RAC0013</strong>.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>ASCOM Conformance Check</th><th>ConformU Debug Log Test</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @for (var tier = 1; tier <= Tiers.Max; tier++)
                        {
                            <tr><td>@Tiers.Name(tier)</td><td>@Tiers.Purpose(tier, false)</td><td>@Tiers.Purpose(tier, true)</td></tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Tier 3 ends with a <strong>Summary</strong> finding for each member: whether it confirms or overturns tier 2's
                    diagnosis and recommendation, the final analysis, and the recommended fix.</p>
                <p>Tiers 2 and 3 are never started by hand: each follows up the run just below it (same device,
                    the options as set for the run), and its report says which run that was. Its findings are the
                    <strong>Diagnose…</strong> group for a conformance run, <strong>ConformU.Diagnose…</strong> for a ConformU
                    run. Reviews add each tier 1 run's deeper findings, the deepest tier's
                    where a check was made at more than one.</p>
                <p>A conformance run's tier 2 also takes apart its other kinds of problem: invalid arguments, members that work
                    when they must refuse (tracking off, during a slew), members that answer while disconnected, SlewSettleTime, AxisRates against CanMoveAxis, DriverVersion and the COM registration. Each
                    tests around the problem (the other values, the sibling members, the states around it) and, when the
                    server's source is set under <strong>Options</strong>, reviews its code: the guards a member calls, lines
                    commented out, where a setting is used. The finding names the core issue and the change to make. The
                    measurements (rate offsets, pulse guiding), the preconditions and the settle time move the mount, so they
                    need <strong>Move the mount</strong>; the others run without it.</p>
                <p>Each diagnosis and the checks it follows up:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Member</th><th>Tests</th><th>Follows up</th><th>What it measures</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var d in Tiers.Diagnoses)
                        {
                            <tr>
                                <td>@d.Tier</td>
                                <td>@d.Member</td>
                                <td class="mono small">@foreach (var key in d.Produces) { <div><a href="help#test-@TestNumbers.For(key)">@TestNumbers.For(key)</a></div> }</td>
                                <td class="mono small">@foreach (var key in d.FollowsUp) { <div><a href="help#test-@TestNumbers.For(key)">@TestNumbers.For(key)</a> @key.Replace("{member}", d.Member)</div> }</td>
                                <td>@d.What</td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h3 id="tier-numbers">Test numbers by tier</h3>
                <p>Every test has its own number (the <strong>No.</strong> column under <a href="help#tests">The tests</a>).
                    Its first digit is its tier: tier 1 tests are numbered from 1001, tier 2 from 2001, tier 3 from 3001.
                    A number stays with its test, and a new test takes the next number of its tier.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Tests</th><th>Numbers</th><th>Referenced by the other tiers</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @for (var tier = 1; tier <= Tiers.Max; tier++)
                        {
                            var numbers = TestCatalog.Entries.Where(e => e.Tier == tier).Select(e => e.Number).Order().ToList();
                            var referenced = TestCatalog.FollowedUp(tier);
                            <tr>
                                <td>@Tiers.Name(tier)</td>
                                <td>@numbers.Count</td>
                                <td class="tier-numbers">@Ranges(numbers)</td>
                                <td>
                                    @foreach (var e in referenced)
                                    {
                                        <div class="mono small"><a href="help#test-@e.Number">@e.Number</a> @e.Group.@e.DisplayId
                                            <span class="muted">→ @string.Join(", ", TestCatalog.FollowedBy(e).Select(d => d.Number))</span></div>
                                    }
                                    @if (referenced.Count == 0) { <span class="muted small">none (the deepest tier)</span> }
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                @foreach (var conformU in new[] { false, true })
                {
                    var chains = TestCatalog.Chains().Where(c => c.ConformU == conformU).ToList();
                    <p>@(conformU ? "ConformU Debug Log Test" : "ASCOM Conformance Check"): each tier 1 test with deeper tests,
                        and the numbers that follow it up at each tier.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Tier 1</th><th>Tier 2</th><th>Tier 3</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            @foreach (var c in chains)
                            {
                                <tr>
                                    <td>@TierTest(c.Test)</td>
                                    <td>@foreach (var e in c.Tier2) { @TierTest(e) }</td>
                                    <td>
                                        @foreach (var e in c.Tier3) { @TierTest(e) }
                                        @if (c.Tier3.Count == 0) { <span class="muted small">none yet</span> }
                                    </td>
                                </tr>
                            }
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                }
                <p class="muted">Every other tier 1 test has no deeper tier yet.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="weights">
                <h2>Test weights</h2>
                <p>Every test has a <strong>weight</strong> from 1 to 10: how much it matters that it passes. It is not how likely it is to fail. A
                    10 is a test the run can't go on after failing, such as the connection or being able to stop the mount; a 1 or 2 is a nicety, such as
                    the format of a version string. Weights decide two things:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Whether the next tier runs.</strong> When a tier finishes, its failed tests (Fail or Error, not Warning) are added up by weight.
                        If any weight-@TestWeights.Critical test failed, or the total is more than @Tiers.MaxProblemsToContinue, the next tier is not run for
                        safety, and the finished dialog says why and names the heaviest failures.</li>
                    <li><strong>The priority of a suggested new test</strong> (the heaviest weight among the tests it relates to), which also sets the order
                        they are listed in.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>A weight is found in this order: the test's own weight, set one by one for the tests that matter most (the connection, stopping the mount,
                    slews, park and home, restoring settings); then a rule for a kind of test (an invalid-value or not-implemented check is 3 or 4, a
                    deprecated one 2, since they are about the driver's manners, not safety); then its group's weight. So most tests have their group's weight and
                    only the important ones are individual. A finding with no test of its own counts as @TestWeights.Unknown. The tests at weight 8 and above (with any changes you made):</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Weight</th><th>Tests</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var level in TestCatalog.Entries.Where(e => e.Weight >= 8).GroupBy(e => e.Weight).OrderByDescending(g => g.Key))
                        {
                            <tr>
                                <td>@level.Key</td>
                                <td class="mono small">@foreach (var e in level.OrderBy(e => e.Number)) { <div><a href="help#test-@e.Number">@e.Number</a> @e.Group.@e.DisplayId</div> }</td>
                            </tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <h3>Adjusting the weights</h3>
                <p>The built-in weights are a starting point. On the <strong>Weights</strong> page (under either test's tabs) every test has its weight in a box: type
                    another from 1 to 10 and it is saved at once and used from the next run. The <strong>Limit</strong> at the top (built in: @TestWeights.DefaultLimit)
                    is what a tier's failed weights may add up to before the next tier isn't run. A weight you changed shows the built-in one beside it and a
                    <strong>Reset</strong> button; <strong>Reset everything</strong> puts them all back. Only what you changed is kept (in <code>test-weights.json</code>
                    beside the reports folder), so a weight you left alone follows the built-in one if a later version improves it. The table above shows the weights
                    as they are now.</p>
                <p>The built-in weights are in <span class="mono">Engine/TestWeights.cs</span>: one table for the individual tests and one for the groups.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="themes">
                <h2>Colour themes</h2>
                <p>The switch at the right of the header picks <strong>Day</strong> (light, for daylight), <strong>Dark</strong> (the default),
                    <strong>Dusk</strong> (dim amber, while your eyes adapt) or <strong>Night</strong> (red only). Night uses deep red because the eye's
                    night vision barely responds to it, so the screen doesn't spoil your dark adaptation. With one hue, the outcomes are told apart by
                    shape instead of colour: a Fail is a solid fill, an Error has a dashed outline and a Warning a thick one. This browser remembers
                    your choice. For the dimmest screen, also turn down the device's brightness or use its night-light or red-screen setting.</p>
                <p><strong>Custom</strong> is your own colours. The <strong>✎</strong> button beside <strong>Custom</strong> (or a double click on Custom) opens <em>Your own colours</em>, with seven pickers (background, text,
                    accent, warning, error, fail and info); panels, lines and hover shades are worked out from them. Start from one of the built-in themes,
                    watch the page change as you move a picker, and check the contrast figure beside each colour: under 4.5 to 1 is hard to read. It also says
                    whether the colours are safe for night vision (almost no green or blue). The palette is kept by this browser; <strong>Export</strong> copies it
                    as one line of text and <strong>Import</strong> reads it back, in another browser or another project.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="server-settings">
                <h2>Server settings</h2>
                <p>Under the server on the Run page, in <strong>Settings</strong>. Each server keeps its own. The checklist under the server
                    shows with a check mark, a warning or an information mark what to know before you start.</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Mount</dt>
                    <dd>Simulator or real mount. GSServer takes different code paths for each, so the recommendations name the right one.</dd>
                    <dt>Site hemisphere</dt>
                    <dd>Northern or southern. The slews' targets follow the mount's SiteLatitude, so a latitude with the wrong sign sends them to the wrong
                        part of the sky. Set this and the run warns if the sign of SiteLatitude disagrees, or if the mount homes at the other pole.</dd>
                    <dt>Flip angle</dt>
                    <dd>Degrees either side of the meridian where the mount can point from either side of the pier, for the flip checks.</dd>
                    <dt>Timeouts</dt>
                    <dd>How long a slew (or park, or find home) and an ordinary call may take before they count as never finished.
                        A real mount's flip can take longer than a minute.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section id="safety">
                <h2>Safety warnings</h2>
                <p>Starting a run that moves the mount asks two things of you first.</p>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>The Safety Warning! dialog.</strong> The app connects to the mount and reads whether it is at the home position. The dialog has numbered steps.
                        <strong>1. Mount position</strong> (for a run that moves the mount) shows a green card when it is at home, with <strong>Check for home</strong> to read it again,
                        or a red card when it isn't, with two choices side by side: <strong>A. Let the mount go home</strong> (<strong>Go home</strong>) or <strong>B. Do it by hand</strong>
                        (put it there, power it off and on, then <strong>Check for home</strong>). <strong>2. Before you start</strong> lists what to check: the OTAs removed, your settings
                        understood, and staying with the mount; and, in red, any setting that looks wrong. Below it, clicking <strong>Start run</strong> accepts responsibility for
                        watching the mount. <strong>Start run</strong> is not available until the mount reports at home. If the mount can't be connected
                        or read, only <strong>Check for home</strong> and <strong>Cancel</strong> are offered. Cancel disconnects the mount; Start run keeps it connected.</li>
                    <li><strong>The warnings before the mount moves.</strong> Before the first test that moves the mount, the run looks at what it found so far
                        (the position, time and site) and whether the mount is already slewing, parked or away from home. If anything looks wrong
                        the run pauses and asks: <strong>Proceed</strong> (run the tests that move the mount anyway), <strong>Skip</strong>
                        (leave those tests out and run the rest) or <strong>Stop</strong> (end the run; the summary is still made).
                        Escape counts as Stop. Turn this off under Options with <em>Safety warnings before the mount moves</em>.</li>
                </ol>
                <p>From the command line there is nobody to ask: <code>--gate stop|skip|proceed</code> says what to do (stop by default) and
                    <code>--no-safety-gate</code> turns the warnings off. A run that was stopped exits with code 3.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="options">
                <h2>Options</h2>
                <p>On the Run tab's <strong>Options</strong> sub-tab. They apply to both the conformance test and the ConformU Debug Log Test.</p>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Move the mount</dt>
                    <dd>Slews, syncs, MoveAxis, PulseGuide, park and find home. Tracking and park state are put back afterwards.</dd>
                    <dt>Write settings back unchanged and try invalid values</dt>
                    <dd>Site, guide rates, tracking rate and settle time. Anything wrongly accepted is put back.</dd>
                    <dt>Call SetPark</dt>
                    <dd>Moves the saved park position to wherever the mount is pointing. Needs <em>Move the mount</em>.</dd>
                    <dt>Alpaca protocol checks</dt>
                    <dd>Alpaca Server only: management API, transaction IDs, error replies, HTTP 400 and discovery.</dd>
                    <dt>Extended checks</dt>
                    <dd>Adds a few minutes: tracking drift (on, off, rate offsets), several clients at once, parallel reads and connect cycles.</dd>
                    <dt>Disconnect at the end</dt>
                    <dd>Checks NotConnected errors. Only possible when no other client holds the device.</dd>
                    <dt>Safety warnings before the mount moves</dt>
                    <dd>On by default. Asks Proceed, Skip or Stop when something looks unsafe before the tests that move the mount. See <a href="help#safety">Safety warnings</a>.</dd>
                    <dt>Tell me when the run finishes or needs my answer</dt>
                    <dd>A short sound, and a browser notification if you allow it, when the run finishes or waits for your answer. This browser remembers the choice.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section id="results">
                <h2>Reading the results</h2>
                <p>The findings sit in the <strong>Report</strong> section, with filters for the outcome, the group and a search. Open a finding to see what ASCOM
                    expects, what the server did, how to fix it and where the code probably is. In a finding, <strong>Copy</strong> puts its check, message, expected and actual values, fix and code location on the clipboard.</p>
                <p>Each finding has an outcome:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge fail">Fail</span></td><td>Breaks the ASCOM rules. Fix it.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge error">Error</span></td><td>The call threw something unexpected, or never answered.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge warning">Warning</span></td><td>Allowed, but likely to trouble some clients.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge pass">Pass</span></td><td>As ASCOM expects.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge info">Info</span></td><td>Worth knowing; not a problem.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><span class="badge">Skipped</span></td><td>Not checked: not supported, or not allowed by the run's options.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>The counts above the list are filters: click one to show that outcome, click more to add theirs, and click one again to take it away; with none picked, every outcome shows.
                    Narrow it further by group or with the search box. Click a finding to open it: the check's ID, what was
                    expected, what came back, <strong>how to fix</strong> it, <strong>where</strong> in the code, and a
                    reference to the ASCOM rule.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="reports">
                <h2>The Reports tab</h2>
                <p>Every finished run, newest first: when, the device and its version, COM or Alpaca, and a bar
                    of fail, warning and pass. Click the device to open the report. The buttons at the end of a row
                    download it as Markdown (ready to paste into an issue) or JSON.</p>
                <p>To delete reports, tick them (or tick <strong>Select all</strong>) and press <strong>Delete checked</strong>.
                    You're asked first; deleted reports can't be brought back.</p>
                <p>A report opens with its totals and duration, the device's identity, the run's options, and the findings.
                    Its buttons download it as Markdown, JSON or <strong>CSV</strong> (opens in Excel), or open
                    <strong>Print / PDF</strong>: the whole report laid out for paper, every problem with its details, ready for
                    the browser's Print or Save as PDF.</p>
                <p>A run whose device stopped answering part way (its COM server crashed, the Alpaca server went away) stops
                    there with one <strong>CommunicationLost</strong> error that holds the last errors seen. The groups that need
                    the device are skipped, and the findings up to that point are kept. Such runs are tagged
                    <em>connection lost</em> in the list.</p>
                <p><strong>Run groups.</strong> At the top of the page, a tier 1 report is grouped with the tier 2 and tier 3 reports that followed it up, directly or
                    through each other, and with its re-runs: a report made by <strong>Re-run tier N</strong> joins the group of the report it re-ran. (Reports made
                    before re-runs were recorded stay in their own groups.) Each group shows its reports as tier badges that open them. Pick one and the list below
                    shows only its reports, and <strong>Reviews</strong> and <strong>Summary</strong> show that group, built on its newest tier 1 report. Click it
                    again to show every report. There are <strong>Open Reviews</strong> and <strong>Open Summary</strong> buttons to go there. With no run group
                    picked, Reviews and Summary list nothing and ask you to pick one. The pick lasts until you change it or close the browser tab. A deeper
                    report whose tier 1 report was deleted makes a group of its own.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="recommendations">
                <h2>The Reviews tab</h2>
                <p>Every check that found a problem, across the saved runs, with the recommended fix and where the code
                    probably is. Only the latest run of each device counts, so a problem fixed since an
                    older run doesn't come back. Each server is listed apart, because the fix is in its own code.</p>
                <p>A check is listed once per server, with the worst result first. Under it: the fix (more than one when
                    runs gave different advice), the source locations, the ASCOM reference and each run that found it,
                    with the expected and actual values. A check with no recorded fix shows those values instead.</p>
                <p>Click a count to show failures, errors or warnings (click several to show more than one, and again to take one away); <strong>No problem</strong> also lists the
                    checks that passed. <strong>Download Markdown</strong> saves the list, ready to paste into an issue.</p>
                <p>Reviews shows one <a href="help#reports">run group</a>, the one picked on the Reports tab: a banner at the top says which, with the
                    group's reports as tier badges. With none picked it lists nothing and points you to the Reports tab. A group's Reviews are built on its
                    newest tier 1 report, with the tier 2 and 3 reports that followed it up.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="ai-summary">
                <h2>Summary</h2>
                <p>A page under each test, next to Reviews, for handing problems to an AI coding agent. It lists <strong>problem areas</strong>:
                    each interface member that behaves wrongly, with the tier 1 problem and what tier 2 and tier 3 found out about its cause, together.
                    Tick the areas you want (<strong>Select all shown</strong> and <strong>Clear</strong> help), then <strong>Download Markdown</strong>, or
                    <strong>Copy</strong> it. <strong>Preview</strong> shows the text first.</p>
                <p>The file is written to be given to the agent as it is. It says what the harness is and how to read an area, then for each area the problem
                    (what the test expected and got), the analysis of the cause, the confirmed cause and the fix when tier 3 has one, the files and lines to change,
                    and the checks that must pass afterwards, with a command to run them again. A line number is a pointer found by searching the server's source,
                    so the agent is told to read around it and judge. Servers are kept apart: the fix goes in the code of the server named in each heading.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="checks">
                <h2>What gets checked</h2>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Checks</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        @foreach (var g in TestRunner.Groups)
                        {
                            <tr><td><strong>@g.Name</strong></td><td>@g.Description</td></tr>
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <section id="tests">
                <h2>The tests</h2>
                <p>Every check a run can make, by group: its objective, how it is tested, how the result is verified, and
                    the reference material it is based on. In IDs, words in braces stand for each member, axis, direction
                    or rate checked. Search, or pick a group, to narrow the list.</p>
                <TestCatalogView />
            </section>

            <section id="source">
                <h2>Where to check the code</h2>
                <p>A finding names the file and line to look at, such as <code>Driver/Telescope.cs:1265</code>, when the
                    server's source code is available. Under the server, on either test's Server card, paste the top folder of
                    the server's code (or its GitHub URL) into <strong>Source Code local Path or GitHub full URL to the code</strong>,
                    or click the folder button beside it to choose it: the popup lists the folders on this computer (start from
                    <code>source\repos</code>, Documents, your home folder or a drive). It can be any server's code: the
                    folder is checked for a telescope driver (a class implementing ITelescopeV4 or ITelescopeV3), and the card
                    says which folder findings will use. The path is your own: it's saved in
                    <code>source-folders.json</code> beside the reports folder, so everyone who runs the harness sets their own,
                    and it's shared by both tests.</p>
                <p>Left blank, there is no code review: findings don't name files, and tier 2 says the code wasn't reviewed.
                    Nothing is looked for beside this program. On the command line, <code>--source &lt;folder or GitHub URL&gt;</code>
                    sets it for that run.</p>
                <p>A <strong>GitHub URL</strong> works too: <code>https://github.com/owner/repo</code>, or
                    <code>…/tree/branch</code> for a branch, tag or commit, and <code>…/tree/branch/folder</code> when the
                    driver is in a folder of the repository. It's downloaded once (as a zip, into
                    <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\GS.TestHarness\github</code>) and read from there; the card says when, and its
                    download button fetches the latest. A private repository needs a GitHub token in the
                    <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> environment variable. <code>--source</code> takes a URL as well.</p>
                <p>The code is only reviewed when it's the server answering's: for an Alpaca server, its name (from the
                    management API) must appear in the source. Another server answering (the ASCOM Alpaca Simulators, say,
                    with another Alpaca Server's source set) gets no file names and no code review, and the findings say why.</p>
                <p>The source adds file names to findings, and lets tier 2 review the code (quoting the lines it found). The
                    checks and the diagnoses work without it; a tier 2 finding then says the code wasn't reviewed.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="cli">
                <h2>From the command line</h2>
                <p>For scripts and build pipelines, <code>--run</code> checks one device, saves the report like a run from
                    this page, and exits:</p>
                <pre><code>GS.TestHarness --run Alpaca --host localhost --port 31416 --device 0 [--motion] [--md report.md]
GS.TestHarness --run COM [--progid ASCOM.GS.Sky.Telescope]</code></pre>
                <p>Other options: <code>--setpark</code>, <code>--no-writes</code>, <code>--no-protocol</code>,
                    <code>--no-disconnect</code>, <code>--extended</code>, <code>--drift-seconds &lt;s&gt;</code>,
                    <code>--slew-timeout &lt;s&gt;</code>,
                    <code>--call-timeout &lt;s&gt;</code>, <code>--flip-angle &lt;degrees&gt;</code>, <code>--mount simulator|real</code>, <code>--hemisphere north|south</code>,
                    <code>--gate stop|skip|proceed</code>, <code>--no-safety-gate</code>, <code>--dry-run</code> (say what the run would do, without connecting)
                    and <code>--source &lt;folder&gt;</code>.</p>
                <p><code>--conformu &lt;debug log&gt;</code> runs the ConformU Debug Log Test instead: only the log's problem members,
                    with the verdicts and diagnoses. For example:</p>
                <pre><code>GS.TestHarness --run COM --motion --mount simulator --conformu ConformU.debug.txt</code></pre>
                <p>The exit code is 0 when nothing failed, 1 when there were failures or errors, 2 for bad arguments and 3 when the run was stopped before it finished.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="files">
                <h2>Where things are stored</h2>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Reports</dt>
                    <dd><code>@Store.Folder</code>, one JSON file per run (setting <code>ReportsFolder</code>).</dd>
                    <dt>Servers</dt>
                    <dd><code>servers.json</code> beside the reports folder. Alpaca passwords
                        are saved encrypted (with DPAPI on Windows) and never written into reports.</dd>
                    <dt>Suggested tests and test weights</dt>
                    <dd><code>suggested-tests.json</code> (the tests runs suggested, see Suggested Tests) and <code>test-weights.json</code> (the weights and
                        limit you changed, see <a href="help#weights">Test weights</a>) beside the reports folder.</dd>
                    <dt>Source folders and options</dt>
                    <dd><code>source-folders.json</code> (each server's source code, see <a href="help#source">Where to check the code</a>)
                        and <code>last-run.json</code> (the server and options last used) beside the reports folder.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>

            <section id="trouble">
                <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
                <dl>
                    <dt>Alpaca Discovery finds nothing</dt>
                    <dd>The server must be on the same network, with discovery on and UDP port 32227 not blocked by a
                        firewall. Otherwise add it with + and its host and port.</dd>
                    <dt>The ASCOM Drivers button is missing</dt>
                    <dd>It's only there on Windows. A COM driver also needs the ASCOM Platform installed.</dd>
                    <dt>The Disconnected checks are skipped</dt>
                    <dd>Another client (a planetarium, a guider, GS Point3D Web) is holding the device. Close it, or
                        untick <em>Disconnect at the end</em>.</dd>
                    <dt>Slews time out</dt>
                    <dd>Raise the slew timeout, or check that the mount (or simulator) is unparked and can reach the targets.</dd>
                    <dt>Findings don't name a file</dt>
                    <dd>The server's source wasn't found: see <a href="help#source">Where to check the code</a>.</dd>
                </dl>
            </section>
        </article>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>A test in the tier tables: its number (linking to it under The tests) and its ID.</summary>
    private static RenderFragment TierTest(CatalogEntry e) => @<div class="mono small"><a href="help#test-@e.Number">@e.Number</a> @e.Group.@e.DisplayId</div>;

    /// <summary>"1001–1201" for a run of numbers; runs separated by commas.</summary>
    private static string Ranges(IReadOnlyList<int> numbers)
    {
        var parts = new List<string>();
        for (var i = 0; i < numbers.Count;)
        {
            var j = i;
            while (j + 1 < numbers.Count && numbers[j + 1] == numbers[j] + 1) j++;
            parts.Add(j == i ? $"{numbers[i]}" : $"{numbers[i]}–{numbers[j]}");
            i = j + 1;
        }
        return string.Join(", ", parts);
    }
}
